[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the userId identify? → The person, keyed by username (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: the mechanism for "works across the app." This one is a real constraint in this repo, not a style preference.

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m — there's no module system in the browser side
  of this app, and the CommonJS code in [38;5;153msrc/[39m is separate Node code that never reaches the page. So a shared identity
  that other forms can read needs a delivery mechanism, and picking it now decides what every future form has to do.

  - [1mES modules[0m — add [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m to the script tag and create [38;5;153msrc/identity.js[39m that [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports. Buys real
    encapsulation, explicit imports, and no globals; costs converting the existing script tag (and the page then needs
    to be served over HTTP, not opened as a [38;5;153mfile://[39m URL — worth knowing if you open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly today). This is
    where you'd want to end up if more forms are coming.
  - [1mSecond script tag, global object[0m — [38;5;153midentity.js[39m loaded before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing something like [38;5;153mwindow.AppIdentity[39m.
    Buys the least disruption, works from [38;5;153mfile://[39m; costs a global and implicit load-order coupling that every new form
    inherits.
  - [1mKeep it inside [38;5;153mapp.js[0m — no new file. Cheapest now; directly contradicts "other forms will need it later," since the
    next form either duplicates the logic or triggers this same decision.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m You've told me more forms are coming, which is precisely the case that pays back the
  one-line [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m change, and it's far cheaper to do now with one caller than after three forms depend on a
  global. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m caveat is the only real cost, and it's a question of how you open the page, not a code change.
